## 2. Sign-in and OTP `[LOCKED]` (D-33, D-34, D-35, D-39, D-44, D-46)

**Mobile number**
- Typing and pasting are cleaned: "+91 98765-43210", "098765 43210" → `9876543210`. A leading 0 or 91 is dropped. More than 10 digits left after that is **not cut** (D-101 o04).
- Checks, most basic first: empty → "Enter your mobile number." · more than 10 digits → "That’s more than 10 digits. Check the number." (shown as soon as it's typed, whatever the first digit; same place and style as the other field errors, D-101 o04) · first digit not 6/7/8/9 → "Mobile numbers start with 6, 7, 8 or 9." (shown as soon as it's typed) · fewer than 10 digits → "Enter all 10 digits." (shown on Send OTP). Once an error shows, it re-checks on every keystroke.
- Back from OTP (**Change** or the phone's back) keeps the number, and it edits normally: the first typed digit does **not** replace it (D-101 o10).
- The sign-up line "New to Truemeds? Sign up" is one copy entry with a `{link}` placeholder; "Sign up" is the link (D-101 o25). On OTP, Terms and Privacy open the D-101 pages, each from one config value (o11).
- After sending, server results show as a notice: not registered (with the sign-up route), offline, couldn't send, too many attempts (button counts down "Try again in m:ss"), account inactive (button becomes **Call Doctor Ops**).
- When the "Try again in m:ss" countdown ends, the "Too many attempts" notice goes away and **Send OTP** works again (D-98).

**OTP**
- 4 digits, keyboard opens on load and stays open while verifying. No auto-verify: the doctor taps **Verify & sign in**.
- The terms line stays during **Verifying…** and **Checking…**, so Verify & sign in never moves (D-81 F5, amends D-44 for those two states). The cooldown line reads "Checking…" alone, with no "Didn’t receive it?", and the boxes stay focused (D-81 F7).
- **Verify never moves (Round 5 B, Round 6 item 3):** one line is reserved under the code boxes. Every state with a one-line message (wrong code, tries left, new code sent, code expired, empty or too short, resend, cooldown) shows its message there, or nothing; Verify & sign in keeps the same place. Notice-box states (offline, locked, couldn't verify, resend failed, too many codes) may move it; accepted.
- Waiting text ("Resend in 0:30") and the Resend code link share one height, so Verify & sign in never moves when the link appears (A-03). The terms line is centred and "Terms & Conditions" never splits (A-01, A-02). "Doctor Ops" and the timer never split across lines (A-17). "New code sent." is green text with no icon, like the errors (A-21).
- Wrong code: digits stay visible with red borders; the first new keystroke replaces the whole code. The message is "Wrong code."; from 2 tries left it counts ("Wrong code. 2 tries left."), and at 1 try left it uses the singular ("Wrong code. 1 try left.") (D-81 F1; no next-step wording, D-53 pt 2).
- **5** server-confirmed wrong codes lock **that mobile number** (not the account) `[LOCKED D-34]`. Lock length `[MOCK 15 min, OPEN OQ-017]`. Change number stays available; another number works only if Doctor Ops approved it for this doctor.
- **4** codes per number per limit window (first code + 3 resends), not reset by restarting sign-in `[LOCKED D-35]`. Window length `[MOCK 10 min, OPEN OQ-017]`.
- Resend waits `[MOCK]` 30 s, then 60 s, then 120 s. Resend line hidden when resends are used up or the number is locked.
- Expired code: Resend is available at once; the expired code can't be verified.
- **Sign-in on a locked number** goes straight to OTP in the locked state (`otp_locked`): code field and Verify off, the lock time and "call Doctor Ops" shown, Change still available (D-81 F6).
- All OTP limits are enforced by the server; the app only shows them.

**Faster sign-in** (D-45, D-47)
- Offered on its own screen after an OTP sign-in on a phone that supports it, while faster sign-in is off: after the first sign-in, then again at each later OTP sign-in until it is turned on, at most 3 times (setting `fasterOfferMax`, D-104; "not available" counts as shown). The footer is the action bar (Round 4, item 1); the content above it scrolls. Subtitle on one line: "Use your screen lock instead of an OTP." (A-06). **Turn on** → phone prompt → Home. **Cancelled** prompt → back to the offer, no message. **Failed** → stays on the offer; "Couldn't turn on faster sign-in." sits in the bar above Try again (A-19) · Try again · Not now (D-61, D-65). **Offline** → "You're offline. Check your connection." (D-66). **Unavailable** (the phone answers "unavailable" at Turn on) → stays on the offer, unchanged (title, subtitle, tile and the three points): the amber warning notice "Faster sign-in isn't available on this phone right now." sits in the bar (same slot as Offline and "Couldn't turn on", A-19), the primary reads **Continue** → Home and Not now is hidden; not enabled, recorded as offered, can be turned on later in Profile (D-93, state `offer_unavailable`). **Not now** → Home; offered again at the next OTP sign-in, up to `fasterOfferMax` times in total (D-104); can be turned on or off in Profile (D-69).
- **Which unlock (D-64):** fingerprint or face if set up on the phone; otherwise the phone's **screen lock** (PIN / pattern / password); if the phone has no screen lock at all, the offer is skipped.
- Returning unlock: two buttons (D-105), **Unlock** (primary) · **Use OTP instead** (quiet); no "Switch account". The phone's own prompt; 3 failed tries `[MOCK]` → the same as Use OTP instead. **Use OTP instead** opens **Sign-in with the remembered number filled in** (cursor at the end, Send OTP ready); no SMS is sent until the doctor taps Send OTP. To change account, the doctor edits the number there. This Sign-in follows the normal Sign-in rules: no Back; the phone's back gesture leaves the app (D-101); reopening the app shows unlock again. The footer is the action bar (Round 4, item 1); the content above it scrolls. If unavailable: "Faster sign-in isn't available on this phone right now." is a standing state, so the neutral notice stays at the top of the panel; **Use OTP instead** is the main button in the bar (A-19) and opens the same prefilled Sign-in.
- Wording follows the phone ("Face ID" / "fingerprint"); never suggest Truemeds stores biometric data.

